## STEP-BY-STEP:
------------------

- Application (Spring Security) user ko authenticate karti hai (username/password ya credentials verify karti hai).

- Authentication successful hone ke baad application Servlet Container (Tomcat) se HttpSession create karwati hai.

- Application user ki session-related information (User ID, Roles, Permissions) HttpSession me store karti hai. 
  By default ye server ki local RAM me store hota hai.

- Tomcat HttpSession ko ek unique Session ID assign karta hai aur apne Session Manager me mapping maintain karta hai:
  Session ID → HttpSession Object

  Session Store

 |---------------------------
 |                          |
 | Session ID : ABC123XYZ   |
 |                          |
 |        |                 |
 |        v                 |
 |                          |
 |  HttpSession Object      |
 |                          |
 |    User = Anurag         |
 |    Role = USER           |           
 |    Last Access Time      |
 |    Creation Time         |
 |                          |
 ----------------------------

- Tomcat response ke saath browser ko ek cookie bhejta hai jisme sirf Session ID hoti hai:
  JSESSIONID = ABC123XYZ

- Browser cookie store kar leta hai. Subsequent requests me browser automatically check karta hai ki
  stored cookie current request ke liye applicable hai ya nahi - Domain, Path, Expiry, Secure, SameSite 
  rules ke basis par. Agar applicable ho, to browser automatically JSESSIONID request ke saath bhej deta hai.

  ## IMPORTANT: Browser ke paas actual session data nahi hota — sirf Session ID hoti hai.

- Tomcat Session Store me Session ID lookup karke corresponding HttpSession retrieve karta hai. 
  Application user ki information aur Roles obtain karke Authorization perform karti hai.

- User ko har request par dobara login karne ki zarurat nahi padti.



