## QUESTION : DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS ME HTTP SESSION KE SAATH KYA PROBLEMS AATI HAIN ?
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Single Server architecture me har request ek hi server par aati hai, aur user ka HttpSession object
bhi usi ek server ki RAM me store hota hai. Isliye server Session ID (jo browser request ke saath bhejta hai) 
se easily corresponding HttpSession retrieve karke user ko identify kar leta hai. 
Ye approach simple aur effective hoti hai.


## PROBLEM 1: HORIZONTAL SCALING
----------------------------------

                     User
                       |
                       |
                  Load Balancer
                 /      |      \
                /       |       \
               v        v        v

          Server-1  Server-2  Server-3
         (Session)   (No)       (No)

Jab hum application ko horizontally scale karte hain, to application ke multiple instances different servers par deployed hote hain.
Ab Load Balancer user ki pehli request kisi ek server (e.g., Server-1) par bhejta hai. 
Us server par application Tomcat ke through  HttpSession object create karwati hai, usse RAM me store karta hai, ek Session ID assign karta hai, 
aur internally Session Manager me us HttpSession object ke against Session ID ko map kar leta hai. 
Lekin same user ki subsequent request Load Balancer kisi doosre server (e.g., Server-2) par bhej sakta hai — jiske paas us user ka HttpSession object nahi hota.
Is wajah se us server par user ki request fail ho sakti hai, aur user ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai.


## PROBLEM 2: SERVER RESTART / SERVER CRASH
---------------------------------------------

By default HttpSession object server ki RAM me store hoti hai. Agar jis server par HttpSession stored hai
wo restart ya crash ho jaye, to us server ki RAM clear ho jaati hai.

Iska result ye hota hai ki saari active HttpSessions destroy ho jaati hain, 
aur users ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai.


## PROBLEM 3: HIGH RAM CONSUMPTION
--------------------------------------

HttpSession object by default server ki RAM me store hoti hai. Har logged-in user ke liye
ek alag HttpSession Object maintain kiya jata hai.

Agar application me lakhs of concurrent active users ho, to server ko lakhs of HttpSession
Objects memory (specifically Heap) me maintain karne pad sakte hain.

Result:
 
- Heap usage increase hoti hai → JVM ko frequent GC run karni padti hai
- GC ke Stop-The-World pauses me Tomcat worker threads kuch samay ke liye pause ho jaate hain → response time badh jaata hai
- GC ke baad bhi Heap insufficient rahe → OutOfMemoryError ka risk




