## QUESTION : DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS ME HTTP SESSION KI LIMITATIONS KO KAISE SOLVE KAR SAKTE HAIN? 
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Traditional HttpSession ki in limitations ko overcome
karne ke liye multiple approaches use ki jaati hain.

Common approaches:

----------------------------------
### APPROACH 1: STICKY SESSIONS
----------------------------------

Sticky Session use karne par Load Balancer jis server par user ki initial request forward karta hai,
usi server par authentication successful hone ke baad application (Tomcat ke through) HttpSession object
create karti hai aur usse RAM me store kar deti hai. Saath hi us HttpSession object ko ek Session ID bhi
assign kiya jaata hai, aur internally Session Manager me ek mapping store kar li jaati hai.

Uske baad Load Balancer user aur backend server ke beech ek mapping maintain kar leta hai.

Uske baad Load Balancer us user ki subsequent requests bhi
usi server par forward karta rehta hai, taaki har request ke liye
corresponding HttpSession available rahe.

Yaani ek user ka session lifecycle generally ek hi server se
associated rehta hai.

Isi wajah se Sticky Sessions, Horizontal Scaling me HTTP session-related 
problem ko kaafi had tak solve kar deti hain.

Lekin is approach ki bhi kuch important limitations hain.


1. SERVER FAILURE
-------------------

Agar User-1 ki HttpSession jis Server-1 ki RAM me stored hai, wo crash ya restart ho jaye, 
to HttpSession destroy ho jaati hai — user ko dobara login karna pad sakta hai ya 
"Session Expired" dikh sakta hai.


2. LOAD IMBALANCE WITH STICKY SESSIONS
---------------------------------------

Suppose initially application do servers par deployed thi.

Users gradually login karte gaye. Sticky Sessions ki wajah se kuch users 
Server-1 se aur kuch Server-2 se associate ho gaye.

Ab traffic aur badha, to company ne existing horizontally scaled setup 
me 2 aur servers add kar diye 


                  Load Balancer
              /      |      |      \
             /       |      |       \
            v        v      v        v

      Server-1  Server-2  Server-3  Server-4


Lekin pehle se logged-in users ki HttpSession abhi bhi active hai (na logout, na timeout), aur unka traffic 
constantly chalta aa raha hai. Sticky Sessions ki wajah se unki future requests ab bhi Server-1 aur Server-2
par hi route hoti hain.

Naye users ka count aur traffic dono kam hai, isliye wo sirf Server-3 aur Server-4 par thoda-thoda distribute hote hain.

Example:

Server-1 : 600 Existing Users
Server-2 : 550 Existing Users
Server-3 :  40 New Users
Server-4 :  35 New Users

Result:

Server-1 aur Server-2 par comparatively zyada load reh jaata hai, jabki Server-3 aur Server-4 underutilized rehte hain. 
Isse overall load balancing efficiency reduce ho jaati hai.



--------------------------------------
### APPROACH 2 : SESSION REPLICATION
--------------------------------------

Sticky Sessions Horizontal Scaling ki session-related problems ko partially solve karti hain.

Lekin is approach ki kuch important limitations ab bhi rehti hain:

1: SERVER FAILURE
2. LOAD IMBALANCE WITH STICKY SESSIONS

In limitations ko overcome karne ke liye Session Replication approach use ki ja sakti hai.

Session Replication me user ki HttpSession object sirf ek server ki RAM me store nahi hoti, 
balki cluster ke sabhi servers ke saath replicate bhi ki jaati hai.

                   
                               Load Balancer
                                     |
        -------------------------------------------------------------------
        |                      |                     |                     |
        |                      |                     |                     |
        |                      |                     |                     |
        |                      |                     |                     |
      Server-1  <--------->  Server-2 <--------->  Server-3  <--------->  Server-4
       Session               Session     Session               Session
           ▲                    ▲                     ▲                     ▲
        Replication         Replication            Replication         Replication



## QUESTION :  SESSION REPLICATION KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI?
-----------------------------------------------------------

Suppose user login karta hai. Load Balancer login request Server-1 par forward karta hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad Server-1 HttpSession object create karta hai, usse ek Session ID assign karta hai,
aur internally Session Manager me us Session ID ko HttpSession object ke against map kar deta hai.

Lekin is baar HttpSession sirf Server-1 ki RAM me store nahi hoti — Server-1 usse background me cluster ke baaki servers
ke saath replicate (synchronize) kar deta hai. Yaani same HttpSession object multiple servers ke respective RAM me available hoti hai.

Replication ke dauran, baaki servers bhi apne Session Manager me us HttpSession object ki copy aur Session ID ki
mapping dono maintain karte hain — bilkul waise hi jaise Server-1 ne ki thi.

Isi wajah se ab agar user ki next request Server-2 par aa jaye, to Server-2 apne Session Store me usi Session ID 
ko lookup karke corresponding HttpSession object retrieve kar leta hai. Server-2 user ko identify kar sakta hai, 
aur dobara login karne ki zarurat nahi padti.



## QUESTION : SESSION REPLICATION STICKY SESSIONS KI LIMITATIONS KO KAISE SOLVE KARTA HAI?
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Server Failure (Single Point of Failure): HttpSession object multiple servers par replicated hoti hai. 
   Isliye ek server crash/restart hone par bhi dusre server par session ki copy available rehti hai, 
   aur user apna session continue kar sakta hai.

2. Load Imbalance: Kyunki ab har server ke paas session available hai, Load 
   Balancer freely available servers ke beech requests distribute kar sakta hai. 
   User kisi ek server se "stuck" nahi rehta.



## QUESTION : SESSION REPLICATION KI LIMITATIONS KYA HAIN?
-------------------------------------------------------------

1. RAM OVERHEAD
-------------------

Har server ko apne HttpSession objects ke alawa dusre servers ke HttpSession objects bhi store karne padte hain (own sessions + replicated sessions).
Is wajah se har server ki RAM consumption badh jaati hai.


2. NETWORK OVERHEAD & POOR SCALABILITY
---------------------------------------

Session Replication me har HttpSession object ko cluster ke multiple servers
ke saath synchronize (replicate) karna padta hai.

Ye replication sirf Session create hone par hi nahi,
balki Session update hone par bhi hoti hai.

## NOTE:
---------

Session Update ka matlab hai HttpSession ke andar stored
session data (attributes) ya session metadata me koi bhi change hona.

Examples:

- User ki Last Access Time update hona.
- User ki Language Preference change hona.
- User ke Roles / Permissions update hona.


## EXAMPLE:
------------

Suppose HttpSession Timeout = 30 min.

User 10:00 AM par login kiya. 10:20 AM par request bheji → Tomcat HttpSession object me Last Access Time update karta hai → ye update bhi cluster ke baaki servers ke saath replicate hoti hai.

Matlab, sirf HttpSession object creation hi nahi, uske updates bhi replication trigger karte hain. Jaise-jaise cluster size badhta hai, replication traffic continuously badhta jaata hai.

Suppose:

50 Servers + 100000 Active Sessions

Har HttpSession object ko itne saare servers ke saath replicate karna practical nahi hota.

## RESULT:
-----------

- Higher Bandwidth Consumption
- Reduced Scalability



3. REPLICATION LAG
-------------------

Suppose User-1 login karta hai. Load Balancer login request Server-1 par forward karta hai.

Authentication successful hone ke baad Server-1 HttpSession object create karta hai, aur usse
cluster ke baaki servers ke saath replicate (synchronize) karna start karta hai.

Lekin Session Replication instant nahi hoti — network communication aur processing time lagta hai.

Isi beech agar User-1 ki next request aa jaye aur Load Balancer usse Server-2 par forward kar de,
to ho sakta hai us samay tak HttpSession object Server-2 par replicate na hui ho.


## RESULT:
-----------

Server-2 corresponding HttpSession object retrieve nahi kar paata, aur application user ko unauthenticated treat kar sakti hai.

Depending on application implementation:

- "Session Expired" message dikhaya ja sakta hai
- Web application me Login Page par redirect
- REST API me 401 Unauthorized

Is temporary delay ko Replication Lag kehte hain.




