## APPROACH 3 : EXTERNAL SESSION STORE (REDIS)
-------------------------------------------------------

Session Replication Horizontal Scaling ki session-related
problems ko solve kar deti hai.

Lekin jaise-jaise application horizontally scale hoti hai,
Session Replication ki apni limitations bhi saamne aati hain:

    - RAM Overhead increase hota hai.
    - Network Traffic increase hota hai.
    - Large clusters me scalability reduce ho sakti hai.
    - Replication delay ki wajah se temporary consistency issues
      aa sakte hain.



## EXTERNAL SESSION STORE KA BASIC IDEA
------------------------------------------

HttpSession object ko har application instance ke server ke RAM me store karne ke bajay, 
ek external shared storage me store kar diya jata hai.

Sabhi application instance servers isi shared session store se HttpSession object 
ko read aur update karte hain.




                    Load Balancer
                   /      |      \
                  v       v       v

            Server-1  Server-2  Server-3
                 \        |        /
                  \       |       /
                   \      |      /
                       Redis


Isse sabhi application instance servers ke across HttpSession objects ko replicate/synchronize
karne ki zarurat nahi hoti. Isliye Session Replication ke:

    - RAM Overhead
    - Replication Overhead
    - Replication Lag

ko avoid kiya ja sakta hai.



## QUESTION : KAUNSI TECHNOLOGY USE KI JA SAKTI HAI?
--------------------------------------------------------

External Session Store ke liye multiple technologies use ki ja sakti hain:

- Redis
- Database
- Hazelcast
- Apache Ignite

Inme se Redis distributed session management ke liye ek very common choice hai.



### QUESTION : REDIS KYUN?
---------------------------

Redis ek in-memory (RAM) data store hai jo high-speed read/write operations provide karta hai.
Isliye frequently accessed session data ke liye Redis suitable hota hai.

Redis TTL (Time-To-Live) support karta hai — session expiration automatically handle ho jaati hai, 
alag cleanup logic ki zarurat nahi.



## QUESTION : REDIS AS AN EXTERNAL SESSION STORE KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose application horizontally scaled hai aur multiple servers par deployed hai.


1: User login karta hai → Load Balancer login request Server-1 par forward karta hai.

2: Server-1 par deployed application instance (using framework like Spring Security) user ko authenticate karti hai, 
   HttpSession object Tomcat ke through create karti hai, aur usse Redis me store kar deti hai.

   Session me required information store ki ja sakti hai,
   for example:

    - User ID
    - Roles
    - Authentication Context
    - Session Metadata


    ## NOTE :
    ----------

    Technically HttpSession object directly Redis me store nahi hota. Redis me sirf ek Key-Value pair store hota hai:

    KEY = Session ID (e.g., ABC123XYZ)
    VALUE = Us session ka data (serialized form me)

    Yaani Redis ke perspective se ye koi HttpSession object nahi — sirf ek key hai jiske against ek serialized data blob pada hai.


3:  HttpSession create hone ke baad ek unique Session ID generate hoti hai aur woh Session ID us HttpSession ko assign kar di jaati hai.

    Example:
      JSESSIONID = ABC123XYZ


4:  Application instance HTTP response ke Set-Cookie header ke through Session ID browser ko bhejti hai. 
    Browser ke paas actual HttpSession data nahi hota — sirf Session ID hoti hai.


5:  Ab user ki next HTTP request aati hai. Browser automatically JSESSIONID cookie request ke saath bhej deta hai.
    Suppose Load Balancer is baar request Server-2 par forward karta hai.

6:  Server-2 par deployed application instance Session ID se Redis me lookup karti hai aur corresponding session 
    data retrieve kar leti hai.


7:  Ab user ki request Server-1, Server-2, ya Server-3 — kisi bhi deployed application instance par ja sakti hai.
    Har instance same Redis Session Store se corresponding session data retrieve kar sakta hai.

Isliye:

    ❌ Sticky Sessions ki zarurat nahi
    ❌ Session Replication ki zarurat nahi

Aur application horizontally scale hone ke baad bhi
session shared state maintain kar sakti hai.





