## QUESTION: JWT KO CLIENT SIDE PAR KAHAN STORE KAREIN?
---------------------------------------------------------

JWT client side par store kiya jata hai, taaki subsequent requests
me server ko bheja ja sake. Do common approaches hain:


1. BROWSER LOCAL STORAGE
--------------------------

Browser JWT ko localStorage me store kar sakta hai, aur JavaScript usse read kar sakti hai.

Agar application me Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability
aa gayi aur attacker JavaScript execute karwa paya, to malicious
JavaScript localStorage se JWT read kar sakti hai.


### QUESTION : WHAT IS XSS (CROSS-SITE SCRIPTING) ?
----------------------------------------------------

XSS me attacker application ke web page me malicious JavaScript execute 
karwane me successful ho jata hai.

Ye malicious JavaScript victim ke browser me run hoti hai aur browser me 
accessible data (jaise localStorage me stored JWT) read karke attacker ko 
bhej sakti hai.

Isliye sensitive tokens ko localStorage me store karne se XSS ke case me 
token theft ka risk hota hai.





## 2. HttpOnly Cookie
-----------------------

JWT ko browser ke HttpOnly Cookie me store kiya ja sakta hai.

Cookie ek chhota data (key-value pair) hota hai jo browser apne storage me rakhta hai, aur matching HTTP requests ke saath automatically Backend ko bhej deta hai.

HttpOnly Cookie = Cookie + HttpOnly attribute.

Set-Cookie:
accessToken=<JWT>;
HttpOnly;
Secure;
SameSite=Lax;


JWT ko browser ke HttpOnly Cookie me store kiya ja sakta hai.


### 2.1 HttpOnly
----------------

Ye attribute browser ko instruct karta hai ki JavaScript is cookie ko directly read nahi kar sakti.

Isliye agar application me XSS vulnerability aa bhi jaye, to malicious JavaScript HttpOnly cookie ko read karke JWT steal nahi kar sakti.


### 2.2 Secure
--------------

Secure attribute browser ko instruct karta hai ki cookie sirf HTTPS requests ke saath bheji jaaye — insecure HTTP par transmit nahi hogi.


### 2.3 SameSite
---------------

Ye attribute control karta hai ki browser
cross-site requests ke saath cookie bhej sakta hai
ya nahi.


#### SAME-SITE  vs CROSS-SITE :
---------------------------------

Browser ke perspective se site mainly domain context ko refer karti hai.

Simple terms me:

    https://example.com
    https://example.com/orders
    https://example.com/profile

Ye same site hain.

Jabki:

    https://example.com
    https://google.com

Ye different sites hain.


Cross-site request = Request FROM ek site se aati hai aur TO doosri site par jaati hai.


#### QUESTION :  CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) KYA HAI?
---------------------------------------------------------------

CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) me attacker victim ke
browser se target application, for example bank.com, par
unwanted request bhejwane ki koshish karta hai.

Suppose user mybank.com par logged in hai. Browser ke paas mybank.com ki ek cookie stored hai (jisme JWT hai).

Ab user kisi doosri website — maan lo evil.com — ko open karta hai.

Attacker evil.com ke page me malicious HTML/JavaScript code daal deta hai. Jaise hi user evil.com open karta hai, 
wo code automatically user ke browser se mybank.com ke kisi endpoint par request trigger karne ki koshish karta hai:

evil.com → POST mybank.com/api/transfer

Ab dhyan do — browser request ke saath mybank.com ki cookie automatically bhej deta hai (kyunki ye mybank.com ke liye hai).

Matlab mybank.com ko lagega ki ye request genuine user ne bheji hai — aur wo transfer execute kar dega. Jabki user ne kuch bhi nahi kiya.

Ye cross-site request hai.

Kyunki request:

    FROM → evil.com
    TO   → mybank.com

Aur:
    evil.com ≠ mybank.com



#### QUESTION : SameSite ISKO KAISE ROKTA HAI?
-------------------------------------------------

SameSite cross-site requests ke saath cookies bhejne ko
restrict karke CSRF risk ko reduce karta hai.


##### SameSite = Strict
--------------------------

- Sabse restrictive option. 
- Browser cross-site context me cookie send nahi karta.



##### SameSite = Lax
----------------------

- Strict se thoda relaxed.
- Cross-site API requests me cookie nahi bhejta,  lekin safe top-level navigation (user kisi link par click kare) me bhej deta hai.

Example:

    evil.com internally automatically calls mybank.com/api/transfer
        → Cookie nahi bhejta ❌

    evil.com → User clicks "Go to mybank.com"
        → Cookie bhejta hai ✅

Isliye Lax ko commonly balanced option maana jata hai.


### SameSite = None
---------------------

Ye attribute browser ko cross-site requests ke saath
bhi cookie send karne ki permission deta hai.

SameSite = None use karne par Secure attribute bhi required hota hai.



